WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.586
This is the second video for week one.

2
00:00:02.636 --> 00:00:05.222
The first video settled what an agent is.

3
00:00:05.272 --> 00:00:06.870
This one is about money.

4
00:00:06.920 --> 00:00:12.143
Where are agents producing value right now, where are pilots stalling, and what separates the two?

5
00:00:12.193 --> 00:00:17.416
You will need this for the opportunity audit your team hands in after the first class.

6
00:00:18.166 --> 00:00:20.089
Start with the uncomfortable number.

7
00:00:20.140 --> 00:00:35.481
In August 2025 a report from MIT's NANDA initiative, based on interviews, a survey and a review of public deployments, concluded that about 95 percent of enterprise generative AI pilots had produced no measurable effect on profit and loss.

8
00:00:35.531 --> 00:00:39.428
Fortune's headline was blunt: 95 percent of pilots are failing.

9
00:00:39.478 --> 00:00:42.191
Two details matter more than the headline.

10
00:00:42.241 --> 00:00:51.663
The pilots that worked were usually bought rather than built in house, and they were pointed at one back-office process with a clear owner.

11
00:00:51.713 --> 00:01:00.346
The ones that stalled were general-purpose tools that never learned from feedback and never fit into how the work was actually done.

12
00:01:01.096 --> 00:01:04.572
Gartner reached a similar conclusion from the other direction.

13
00:01:04.622 --> 00:01:18.679
In June 2025 it predicted that more than 40 percent of agentic AI projects would be canceled by the end of 2027, and it gave three reasons: rising cost, unclear business value, and weak risk controls.

14
00:01:18.729 --> 00:01:24.948
Hold on to those three, because they map onto the four questions from the first video.

15
00:01:24.998 --> 00:01:26.516
Cost is question four.

16
00:01:26.566 --> 00:01:29.259
Business value is question three, measured honestly.

17
00:01:29.309 --> 00:01:32.002
Risk controls are questions one and two.

18
00:01:32.052 --> 00:01:34.745
Notice what is not on the list.

19
00:01:34.795 --> 00:01:37.487
Nobody said the models were too weak.

20
00:01:38.237 --> 00:01:43.578
Now the good news, and it comes from a careful study rather than a vendor.

21
00:01:43.628 --> 00:01:51.124
Brynjolfsson, Li and Raymond followed more than five thousand customer-support agents at a software company after an AI assistant was introduced.

22
00:01:51.174 --> 00:01:55.795
Productivity, measured as issues resolved per hour, rose about 14 percent on average.

23
00:01:55.845 --> 00:01:59.388
For the least experienced workers it rose about 34 percent.

24
00:01:59.438 --> 00:02:01.545
The most experienced workers gained little.

25
00:02:01.595 --> 00:02:03.701
Two things to take from this.

26
00:02:03.751 --> 00:02:13.043
First, the clearest wins so far are in assisted work, where a person stays in the loop and the AI drafts, suggests and looks things up.

27
00:02:13.093 --> 00:02:23.105
Second, the value went mostly to novices, which tells you where to point an agent: at work where the knowledge of the best people can be written down.

28
00:02:23.855 --> 00:02:26.143
The capability side is moving fast.

29
00:02:26.193 --> 00:02:38.614
In September 2025 OpenAI released GDPval, a benchmark built from 1,320 real work products across 44 occupations, each written and graded by professionals with about 14 years of experience.

30
00:02:38.664 --> 00:02:44.849
Expert graders compared the model's output with the professional's own work without knowing which was which.

31
00:02:44.899 --> 00:02:53.033
The best model at the time was rated as good as or better than the professional in 47.6 percent of comparisons.

32
00:02:53.083 --> 00:02:54.982
Read the fine print, though.

33
00:02:55.032 --> 00:03:01.997
Every GDPval task arrives with a clear brief and all the files the model needs.

34
00:03:02.047 --> 00:03:07.453
In a company, finding the brief and the files is most of the job.

35
00:03:07.503 --> 00:03:12.519
So GDPval tells you the writing and analysis hold up.

36
00:03:12.569 --> 00:03:17.975
It does not tell you the agent will find its way around your organization.

37
00:03:18.725 --> 00:03:21.624
Put the evidence together and a pattern appears.

38
00:03:21.674 --> 00:03:33.419
Agents fit where one user does one workflow with a bounded set of tasks, where the result can be checked, and where mistakes can be undone or a person reviews them first.

39
00:03:33.469 --> 00:03:37.474
Customer support, document drafting, data pulls, internal help desks, first-pass reviews.

40
00:03:37.524 --> 00:03:51.112
They fit badly where the goal is open-ended and nobody can say what done looks like, where actions are irreversible and unsupervised, and where the knowledge needed lives only in people's heads and was never written down.

41
00:03:51.862 --> 00:03:53.713
That pattern is your first deliverable.

42
00:03:53.763 --> 00:03:57.516
The opportunity audit asks five things about the job your team picks.

43
00:03:57.566 --> 00:04:01.319
Who does it today, how often, and how long does it take.

44
00:04:01.369 --> 00:04:05.121
Who the user is and what the bounded list of tasks is.

45
00:04:05.171 --> 00:04:08.290
What done looks like, in a form you can check.

46
00:04:08.340 --> 00:04:11.459
What goes wrong today, and how costly a mistake is.

47
00:04:11.509 --> 00:04:15.578
And how much autonomy you would allow at first: suggest, draft, or act.

48
00:04:15.628 --> 00:04:19.064
If you cannot answer the third question, pick a different job.

49
00:04:19.114 --> 00:04:25.401
The next video opens up the harness, the code around the model, which is where most of this course lives.
